Create a PowerPoint deck (16:9 widescreen) in a clean, minimalist “warm paper” system-diagram style. Keep everything crisp, high-whitespace, and highly readable. No gradients, shadows, glow, textures, or transparency.

DESIGN TOKENS (use exactly):

* Slide background: #EEEAE3 (warm paper)
* Panel/card fill: #FAF7F2
* Panel/card border: #E7E2DA (thin 1–1.5pt)
* Primary text: #1F1F1F
* Secondary text: #5F5F5A
* Neutral strokes/arrows: #6C6B66 (thin 1–1.5pt)
* Optional dashed boundary stroke: #8C8C82 (dashed 2–3–2)

ACCENT PALETTE (use sparingly; 1–2 accents per slide; rotate across deck):

* Blue: fill #ACCEF2, stroke/text #1F4173
* Mint: fill #A4E3C9, stroke/text #235F46
* Orange: fill #F8DAA7, stroke/text #CD5F37
* Lavender: fill #EBD9F2, stroke/text #4B2F6B

TYPOGRAPHY (Office-safe, consistent hierarchy):

* Title: Aptos Display, 34pt, Bold, #1F1F1F
* Subtitle / lead-in: Aptos, 18pt, #5F5F5A
* Body bullets: Aptos, 16–20pt, #1F1F1F, generous line spacing
* Diagram labels: Aptos, 14–16pt, Bold (use accent stroke/text color)
* Small tags/pills: Aptos, 12pt, Bold (use accent stroke/text color)

GRID & SPACING (this is what makes it “polished”):

* Outer margins: ~0.7" on all sides (strict)
* Left-aligned text blocks; avoid center alignment except for diagram labels
* Use consistent padding inside cards (visually uniform across slides)
* Prefer 1–3 large cards per slide (max 4); avoid clutter
* Spacing rhythm should feel uniform (consistent gaps between elements)

COMPONENTS (reusable primitives):

1. Cards/Panels:

   * Rounded rectangle (medium corner radius)
   * Fill #FAF7F2, border #E7E2DA, thin stroke
   * Title in Aptos Bold; body in Aptos regular
2. Diagram Nodes:

   * Rounded rectangles with neutral border (#6C6B66) OR accent fill + accent stroke
   * Labels bold; keep node sizes consistent within a diagram
3. Problem/Feature Pills:

   * Small rounded pills; use neutral fill (#FAF7F2) with accent border/text OR full accent fill
   * Keep pills aligned and evenly spaced
4. Arrows/Connectors:

   * Thin #6C6B66; small arrowheads; minimal crossings
   * Use dashed outline only for “optional/outer boundary” concepts
5. Legend/Mapping Chips:

   * Small accent chips (fill is accent, text is darker accent) placed in a single row/stack

DIAGRAM STYLE RULES:

* Avoid heavy wiring; keep lines short and orthogonal when possible
* Use whitespace to separate clusters instead of thick borders
* If multiple layers are shown (Problems → Solutions → Vendor mapping), layer them vertically with clear spacing, not overlapping.

OUTPUT EXPECTATION:

* Slides must look like a cohesive template: same background, same margins, same card style, same font hierarchy, and consistent accent usage.
* Professional, elegant, readable at a distance.
